<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_block — blocked-user icon (circle with diagonal slash)
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - fragment_profile.xml (row_blocked leading affordance)
      - future: ChatActivity overflow menu ("Block user")
      - future: block-confirmation dialog icon

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (ImageView's app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant").
       Do NOT set a brand color here — it would be tinted
       away, but would mislead future maintainers.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app.

    3. UNIVERSAL "PROHIBITED" GLYPH.
       A circle with a diagonal slash through it is the
       universal "not allowed / blocked / prohibited" symbol
       (same as a "no entry" traffic sign). It is instantly
       recognized in every locale, including for RTL users
       and users unfamiliar with English.

       Do NOT redesign this as, for example, a user
       silhouette with an X through it. While that is
       semantically closer to "blocked user," it is a
       custom composition that would not be recognized at
       24dp and would look visually inconsistent with the
       other Material-standard icons in the settings rows.
       The circle-with-slash is the standard Material
       `block` glyph and is the right choice for v1.

    4. FILLED RING + FILLED SLASH, BOTH UNDER evenOdd.
       The icon is composed of two contours in the SAME
       <path> element with fillType="evenOdd":
         - Outer circle (the ring's exterior)
         - Inner circle (the ring's interior / hole)
       This produces the hollow ring.
       The slash is a SEPARATE <path> element (a filled
       parallelogram rotated 45°) drawn AFTER the ring, so
       it sits on top of the ring's hollow interior and
       crosses both sides of the ring's stroke, extending
       slightly beyond the outer circle.

       The slash overlap with the ring's stroke does not
       produce any visual seams because the slash is the
       SAME fill color as the ring (and under the same
       tint). This is the standard construction for the
       Material `block` icon and produces a clean result
       at any size.

    5. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The circle-slash is not directional (a mirrored
       circle-slash looks identical). Do not add
       auto-mirroring.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        ============================================================
        RING (outer circle minus inner circle, evenOdd)
        ============================================================
        Outer circle: center (12,12), radius 10 → spans (2,2)
                      to (22,22)
        Inner circle: center (12,12), radius 8  → spans (4,4)
                      to (20,20)
        Ring stroke:  2 units thick

        The 2-unit stroke matches every other chrome icon's
        stroke weight. The A-command (arc) syntax is used for
        both circles (same as ic_nav_profile.xml's outer circle).

        Path:
          M 2,12 A10,10 0 1,0 22,12 A10,10 0 1,0 2,12 Z
          M 4,12 A8,8 0 1,1 20,12 A8,8 0 1,1 4,12 Z

        The two contours are both written as "double-arc
        circles" (two A commands tracing a full circle). The
        sweep direction is reversed between them (0 then 1)
        which, combined with evenOdd, produces the ring
        regardless of the source winding — the evenOdd rule
        is winding-direction-agnostic. This is more robust
        than relying on same-direction windings that could
        be flipped by a build-tool optimization.
        ============================================================
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M2,12 A10,10 0 1,0 22,12 A10,10 0 1,0 2,12 Z M4,12 A8,8 0 1,1 20,12 A8,8 0 1,1 4,12 Z" />

    <!--
        ============================================================
        SLASH (diagonal parallelogram, drawn over the ring)
        ============================================================
        A rectangular strip rotated 45°, from the upper-right
        to the lower-left of the icon. The slash extends from
        approximately (2.5, 3.5) at the top-right to
        (3.5, 2.5) — i.e., beyond the ring's outer edge on
        both ends, so the slash clearly crosses the ring.

        Slope: 45° (from upper-right to lower-left, matching
               the "no entry" traffic sign's slash direction)

        Width: 2 units (same as the ring stroke, so the
               slash and the ring appear to have the same
               line weight)

        Construction: a filled parallelogram, not a stroked
        line. Filled is more reliable across OEM renderers
        at small sizes — a stroked diagonal at 24dp sometimes
        renders with sub-pixel anti-aliasing artifacts on
        older GPU drivers.

        Path (parallelogram vertices):
          Top-right corner:     (22.4, 3.6)
          Top-left corner:      (20.4, 1.6)
          Bottom-left corner:   (1.6, 20.4)
          Bottom-right corner:  (3.6, 22.4)
          Close back to top-right.

        This produces a diagonal strip whose center-line
        runs from (21.4, 2.6) to (2.6, 21.4), i.e., exactly
        through the icon's center (12, 12).
        ============================================================
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M22.4,3.6 L20.4,1.6 L1.6,20.4 L3.6,22.4 Z" />

</vector>
